FOPEN(3)                       P32W Unix                        FOPEN(3)

NAME
       fopen, fdopen, freopen, fclose, fflush, remove - open streams

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       FILE *fopen(const char *path, const char *mode);
       FILE *fdopen(int fd, const char *mode);
       FILE *freopen(const char *path, const char *mode, FILE *f);
       int fclose(FILE *f);
       int fflush(FILE *f);
       int remove(const char *path);

DESCRIPTION
       fopen opens the file path and returns a stream for it. The
       first character of mode says how:

       r      read; the file must exist
       w      write; the file is created (mode 0666, less the
              umask) or truncated to nothing
       a      append; the file is created if need be, and every
              write goes to its end (O_APPEND)

       A + anywhere in mode opens the file for reading and writing
       as well ("r+", "w+", "a+"). Other characters, such as the b
       of "rb", are ignored: text and binary are the same.

       fdopen makes a stream for the descriptor fd, already open
       (from open(2), pipe(2), dup(2)), taking mode as fopen does
       but opening nothing; the mode should match how fd is open.

       freopen closes the stream f and opens path on it instead, as
       fopen would; the stream keeps its descriptor number, so
       freopen(path, "r", stdin) makes path the standard input of
       commands the program runs. If path cannot be opened, f is
       closed.

       fclose flushes the stream, closes its descriptor and frees
       its buffer; the FILE is free for the next fopen. It can close
       stdin, stdout and stderr too, which then stay unusable.

       fflush writes out the buffered output of a stream open for
       writing; for a stream open only for reading it does nothing.
       fflush(NULL) flushes every stream open for writing.

       remove deletes the name path: a file (unlink(2)), or an empty
       directory (rmdir(2)).

RETURN VALUE
       fopen and fdopen return the new stream, and freopen returns
       f; each returns NULL with errno set if it fails. fclose and
       fflush return 0, or EOF if a write (or, for fclose, the
       close) failed. remove returns 0, or -1 with errno set.

ERRORS
       fopen fails with any error of open(2), and with
       EINVAL     mode does not start with r, w or a
       EMFILE     all FOPEN_MAX (16) streams are in use

       fdopen fails with EMFILE, or returns NULL for a bad mode
       without setting errno. When a write fails, fflush sets the
       stream's error flag (ferror(3)) and the unwritten output is
       lost.

READING AND WRITING
       A stream opened with + has one buffer, which holds either
       read-ahead or output, never both. Writing after reading
       first moves the file offset back over what was read ahead
       but not used, so the bytes go where the next read would
       have come from; reading after writing first writes the
       output out. As C requires, call fseek (or fflush, after
       writing) between the two for portable programs; P32W's
       library does not depend on it.

EXAMPLES
       FILE *f = fopen("/etc/passwd", "r");
       if (f == NULL) { perror("/etc/passwd"); exit(1); }
       ...
       fclose(f);

SEE ALSO
       open(2), close(2), unlink(2), stdio(3), getc(3), putc(3),
       fseek(3), ferror(3)

BUGS
       fdopen does not check fd.
